"Right now a computer near you may be playing music, showing a web page, and waiting for someone to type. Yet in Lesson 6 you saw that a processor carries out one instruction at a time. So how can it do three things at once? The answer is that it does not. It only looks that way.": "75d4786a6b16",
"Right now a computer near you may be playing music, showing a web page, and waiting for someone to type. Yet in Lesson 6 you saw that a processor carries out one instruction at a time. So how can it do three things at once? The answer is that it does not. It only looks that way. There is one program in charge of all the others: the operating system. An encyclopedia describes it as a program that manages a computer’s resources, especially the way they are shared among other programs. The resources are the processor, the memory, the storage, and the devices. Windows, Android, and iOS are operating systems. Think of one chef with four orders. The chef cannot cook them all at once, so the chef works on one for a moment, puts it down, and moves to the next. The operating system does the same with the processor. Each program is allowed to use it for a limited time, perhaps a fraction of a second. Then it must give up control and wait for its next turn. The turns come around so quickly that every program seems to be running all the time. Switching is not free. While the chef is putting one order down and picking up the next, nothing is being cooked. In the same way, switching between programs uses the processor too. If the turns are very long, a program at the back of the line waits a long time, and music would break up. If the turns are very short, too much time goes to switching. The operating system has to find a balance. Sharing the processor is only one of its jobs. The operating system also decides which program gets which part of memory, and it keeps programs from interfering with each other. When memory fills up, it is the operating system that moves things out to storage, as you saw in Lesson 7. It keeps track of where every file is. And it stands between programs and devices: in Lesson 8, it was the operating system that turned a key number into a character. Most programs finish a task and stop. An operating system keeps running until the computer is turned off. The first computers had none: they ran one program at a time, and a human operator looked after everything else. Today many computers have several processor cores, so a few things really can happen at the same moment. But there are usually many more programs than cores, so taking turns is still how it works.": "2ca9159ead81",
"Sharing the processor is only one of its jobs. The operating system also decides which program gets which part of memory, and it keeps programs from interfering with each other. When memory fills up, it is the operating system that moves things out to storage, as you saw in Lesson 7. It keeps track of where every file is. And it stands between programs and devices: in Lesson 8, it was the operating system that turned a key number into a character.": "463cebc79f43",
